1
00:00:00,030 --> 00:00:01,800
In this video, I'm going to run through 14

2
00:00:01,800 --> 00:00:03,360
different types of aircraft to tell

3
00:00:03,360 --> 00:00:04,900
the story of how we learned to fly.

4
00:00:04,900 --> 00:00:06,150
So let's go through them one by one

5
00:00:06,150 --> 00:00:07,500
and look at how they were designed.

6
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:08,900
Starting off, we have the glider.

7
00:00:08,900 --> 00:00:10,690
Before anyone put an engine on a plane,

8
00:00:10,690 --> 00:00:12,230
someone had to prove that a wing

9
00:00:12,230 --> 00:00:13,600
could actually hold a person up.

10
00:00:13,600 --> 00:00:15,290
And that person was Otto Lilienthal,

11
00:00:15,290 --> 00:00:17,300
a German engineer in the 1890s.

12
00:00:17,300 --> 00:00:19,140
He built his own gliders, which were big

13
00:00:19,140 --> 00:00:20,510
curved wings made of fabric,

14
00:00:20,510 --> 00:00:21,180
kind of like bat wings.

15
00:00:21,200 --> 00:00:22,750
And he would hold onto the glider, run off

16
00:00:22,750 --> 00:00:24,200
the top of a hill, and glide down.

17
00:00:24,200 --> 00:00:26,160
And he did this over and over, about 2,000

18
00:00:26,160 --> 00:00:26,900
times in total.

19
00:00:26,930 --> 00:00:28,690
He even had a hill built for this exact

20
00:00:28,690 --> 00:00:30,900
purpose, which was about 15 meters tall.

21
00:00:30,900 --> 00:00:32,200
Just so he could launch into the wind

22
00:00:32,200 --> 00:00:33,100
from any direction.

23
00:00:33,100 --> 00:00:35,500
His longest glides went about 250 meters.

24
00:00:35,500 --> 00:00:36,970
So his gliders had pretty much no

25
00:00:36,970 --> 00:00:37,900
controls at all.

26
00:00:37,900 --> 00:00:40,060
So he wasn't really able to steer, besides

27
00:00:40,060 --> 00:00:41,110
when he swung his legs around

28
00:00:41,110 --> 00:00:42,100
and moved his body around.

29
00:00:42,100 --> 00:00:43,750
And that shifted the weight and the glider

30
00:00:43,750 --> 00:00:45,100
would tilt in that direction.

31
00:00:45,100 --> 00:00:46,500
And it's basically how a hang glider

32
00:00:46,500 --> 00:00:47,200
works today.

33
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:48,810
He also wrote down everything he learned,

34
00:00:48,810 --> 00:00:50,100
and one of his biggest findings

35
00:00:50,100 --> 00:00:51,760
was that a curved wing makes much

36
00:00:51,760 --> 00:00:53,200
more lift than a flat one.

37
00:00:53,200 --> 00:00:54,810
And that curve is called camber,

38
00:00:54,810 --> 00:00:56,500
and almost every wing today has it.

39
00:00:56,530 --> 00:00:58,380
He published tables of how much lift

40
00:00:58,380 --> 00:01:00,590
a curved wing makes at different angles,

41
00:01:00,590 --> 00:01:01,920
and that gave the people after him

42
00:01:01,920 --> 00:01:03,000
something to build on.

43
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:04,730
But obviously steering with your body only

44
00:01:04,730 --> 00:01:06,000
works up to a certain point.

45
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:07,640
The design problem is that as you make

46
00:01:07,640 --> 00:01:09,470
the wing bigger, the forces on it grow

47
00:01:09,470 --> 00:01:11,040
much faster than your ability to move

48
00:01:11,040 --> 00:01:11,900
your weight around.

49
00:01:11,900 --> 00:01:13,040
So a bigger glider can carry

50
00:01:13,040 --> 00:01:14,430
more, but it's also just much

51
00:01:14,430 --> 00:01:15,300
harder to control.

52
00:01:15,300 --> 00:01:18,040
In 1896, Lilienthal lost control, fell

53
00:01:18,040 --> 00:01:20,080
about 15 meters, he broke his spine,

54
00:01:20,080 --> 00:01:21,400
and he died the next day.

55
00:01:21,430 --> 00:01:22,640
So today, these gliders are

56
00:01:22,640 --> 00:01:23,650
called sailplanes.

57
00:01:23,650 --> 00:01:25,450
They have really long, skinny wings,

58
00:01:25,450 --> 00:01:27,000
some over 25 meters across.

59
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:29,160
And this shape basically means that every

60
00:01:29,160 --> 00:01:30,900
wing leaks air around its tips,

61
00:01:30,900 --> 00:01:33,500
which creates swirls called tip vortices.

62
00:01:33,500 --> 00:01:35,110
And these swirls cause a bit of drag.

63
00:01:35,110 --> 00:01:37,380
Basically, a long, narrow wing has less

64
00:01:37,380 --> 00:01:39,500
tip compared to the rest of the wing,

65
00:01:39,500 --> 00:01:41,100
so it wastes a lot less energy.

66
00:01:41,100 --> 00:01:43,050
And engineers will call this, like, a high

67
00:01:43,050 --> 00:01:43,900
aspect ratio.

68
00:01:43,900 --> 00:01:46,030
So pilots are able to find pockets of warm

69
00:01:46,030 --> 00:01:47,700
air that rise off the ground,

70
00:01:47,700 --> 00:01:48,600
and they ride them upwards.

71
00:01:48,630 --> 00:01:50,020
And that means that they can stay up

72
00:01:50,020 --> 00:01:51,500
for hours without an engine.

73
00:01:51,500 --> 00:01:53,110
And the best sailplanes travel more than

74
00:01:53,110 --> 00:01:54,310
50 meters forward for every

75
00:01:54,310 --> 00:01:55,300
meter that they drop.

76
00:01:55,300 --> 00:01:56,980
There's one pilot that has flown over

77
00:01:56,980 --> 00:01:59,350
3,000 kilometers in a single flight,

78
00:01:59,350 --> 00:02:01,320
and a special research glider has climbed

79
00:02:01,320 --> 00:02:03,200
above 23,000 meters.

80
00:02:03,200 --> 00:02:04,860
So gliders basically proved that flying

81
00:02:04,860 --> 00:02:06,130
was possible, but they had two

82
00:02:06,130 --> 00:02:06,900
big problems.

83
00:02:06,900 --> 00:02:08,300
There was no proper way to steer,

84
00:02:08,300 --> 00:02:09,550
and there was no engine to keep

85
00:02:09,550 --> 00:02:10,400
them in the air.

